title: Second mounts
tags: [[Mount points]]
kind: reference
summary: A filesystem, or part of one, mounted a second time inside a repository is an entry, never walked: tracked once, where it is reached first.

A drive bound at two directories of a repository, or a directory of the repository bound
into it again, shows the same files at two paths. The kernel tells them apart nowhere: a
fanotify record names an object, not a mount, and the broker resolves it through one
mount point; an inotify watch is on an inode, which both paths name. So the watcher could
only ever report one of the two, and a reconcile walking both kept a second record of
every file that nothing would keep up to date.

So a repository reaches each part of a filesystem ''once''. A mount point under the root
is a ''second mount'' when what it shows is reached already — through the root itself
(a directory of the root's own filesystem bound into it), or through a mount made before
it, in the order of the kernel's mount table (a whole drive mounted again, or a
directory of it). Like a symlinked directory, a second mount is an entry, tracked as a
directory, and never entered: not walked by the reconcile nor by the watch placement,
events beneath it dropped, its records "unknown" to the orphan scan. What it shows is
tracked where it is first reached, and a change made through it is recorded there.

|!Component |!At a second mount |
|reconcile |the directory itself is tracked; nothing beneath it is walked |
|watcher |no watch beneath it; an event beneath it is dropped |
|orphan scan |"unknown", never an orphan |
|watch check |`second_mount`, naming it ([[Checking whether a path is watched]]) |

It is decided afresh from the mount table each time it is asked (`mount::offline`,
`MountTable::second_mounts_under`): the first mount unmounted, the second is the drive's
one mount now — live, and found by a reconcile as a drive plugged in is
([[Offline subtrees]]); the first one, a declared mount point with nothing mounted, is
frozen.

''A drive in two repositories'' is the same question between repositories: each tracks
it at its own mount point. The broker resolves a record through one mount of the
filesystem and then tells the event again at every other mount showing that path — the
whole drive, or the part of it a bind shows (`Aliases`, from the mount table read at each
resync) — so each subscriber hears of it under its own root, and a repository holding
both mounts receives the second path too, which it drops as above. Under inotify each
repository has a watch of its own on the inode.

!! Not covered: a later mount showing more

A mount showing ''more'' than one made before it is not a second mount, and the part
they share is reached twice. A drive's `photos` directory bound at `/repo/p`, then the
whole drive mounted at `/repo/d`: `/repo/d` is walked and watched — most of it is
reached nowhere else — and so is `/repo/d/photos`, which shows what `/repo/p` shows.
Each photo then has two records, one at each path. The same goes for the root's own
filesystem bound into the repository from above the root (`/` bound at `/repo/rootfs`
reaches the repository again inside it).

The duplicate is an ordinary directory inside a mount, not a mount point, and a second
mount is a mount point of the table: there is nothing to mark it by. Kept as a limit
(the case is rare), not handled: mount the larger part first — the drive at `/repo/d`,
then `photos` at `/repo/p` is the covered order — or leave the smaller one out of the
repository (`mf_watch = false` on it, or an `mf_ignore` pattern).

`tests/watch_mounts.rs` drives one drive at two points of a repository and in two
repositories, under both sources.

